## How Solitaire works
// The Solitaire example is Klondike, the card game that came with every computer:
// seven columns of cards, built down from king to ace, red and black in turn.
// It's the biggest example, but it's made of small pieces:
// - a CARD, which knows its rank, its suit, and from those its color and its value
// - PILES of cards, each kind with its own rules for what it will take
//
// This guide is one card type and one rule:  a column, with a king on it, and four cards to try.
// It's written in the outline style;  Solitaire itself is spellml, the same language in tags.
// Open Solitaire, in the Examples, to play the whole game.


## A card
// "one of" lists every value a property can have:  "2 ... 10" means every number in between
a "card" is a view where:
	- its "rank" is one of ace, 2 ... 10, jack, queen, king
	- its "suit" is one of clubs, diamonds, hearts or spades

	// ace is 1, 2 is 2 ... king is 13:  its place in the list of ranks
	- its "value" is the position of its rank
	- its "color" is:
		red if its suit is diamonds or hearts
		black otherwise

	// e.g. "queen of hearts"
	- its "name" is its rank + " of " + its suit

	// a click tries it on the column
	- to draw it:
		return <ui-button appearance="basic" color={its color} onClick={try it}>{its name}</ui-button>


## A pile, and a column
a "pile" is a viewable list of cards
// a card is in one pile at a time:  putting it on another takes it off this one
a card belongs to one pile

// The rule that makes it Solitaire:  a column takes a card one lower than its last, in the other color.
// - "can take a card when" is a yes-or-no question the pile answers for each card
// - "return" gives the answer:  the first line that returns, wins
a "column" is a pile where:
	- it can take a card when:
		if it is empty
			return the rank of the card is king
		return the color of its last card is not the color of the card and the value of its last card is the value of the card + 1


## The table:  a column, and a hand of cards to try on it
table is an app where:
	- its "column" is a new column
	- its "hand" is a new pile
	- its "message" is text

	- to draw it:
		return <ui-container text>
			<ui-header size="small">The column</ui-header>
			{draw its column}
			<ui-header size="small">Your hand: click a card to put it on the column</ui-header>
			{draw its hand}
			<ui-text paragraph>{its message}</ui-text>
		</ui-container>

// onto the column, if it takes the card;  the message says what happened
// - "table's column" is "the column of table":  either reads the table's property
to try (a card):
	if table's column can take the card
		add the card to table's column
		set table's message to "The " + the card's name + " goes on."
	otherwise
		set table's message to "The column won't take the " + the card's name + "."


## Start:  a king of spades on the column
add (a new card with rank = king, suit = spades) to table's column
add (a new card with rank = queen, suit = spades) to table's hand
add (a new card with rank = queen, suit = hearts) to table's hand
add (a new card with rank = jack, suit = clubs) to table's hand
add (a new card with rank = 10, suit = diamonds) to table's hand


## A check:  the rule, card by card
to test the column
	set the stack to a new column
	set the red queen to a new card with rank = queen, suit = hearts
	the stack can take the red queen should be no
	add (a new card with rank = king, suit = spades) to the stack
	the stack can take the red queen should be yes
	the stack can take (a new card with rank = queen, suit = clubs) should be no


## Try it
// - Click the queen of spades on the right:  the column won't take it, because it's black, like the king.
// - Click the queen of hearts, then the jack of clubs, then the 10 of diamonds:  each one goes on.
// - Bend the rule:  in "a column is a pile where", take out
//   "the color of its last card is not the color of the card and".
//   Then the queen of spades goes on too.  (Click ↻, above the table, to deal the hand again.)
//
// Where next:  Solitaire, in the Examples, where the stock, the discards and the four foundations
// each have rules of their own:  a column's is its "<droppable when>" there.
// Its moves are actions with a title, an icon and a key, drawn as its toolbar and its right-click menu.
// Solitario is the same game, in Spanish.
